2026-06-30 15:51:41,603 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-63e6d3b4-fe4e-4f40-a33a-6a97717069ca', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nE240906_01_AMC_HSA_340B_142_IV_pepSEC_A5_Inj1.raw\nE240906_02_AMC_HSA_340B_142_IV_pepSEC_A6_Inj1.raw\nE240906_03_AMC_HSA_340B_142_IV_pepSEC_A7_Inj1.raw\nE240906_05_AMC_HSA_340B_142_IV_pepSEC_A8_Inj1.raw\nE240906_12_AMC_HSA_340B_142_IV_pepSEC_A5_FAIMS_-55CV_Inj2.raw\nE240906_13_AMC_HSA_340B_142_IV_pepSEC_A6_FAIMS_-55CV_Inj2.raw\nE240906_14_AMC_HSA_340B_142_IV_pepSEC_A7_FAIMS_-55CV_Inj2.raw\nE240906_16_AMC_HSA_340B_142_IV_pepSEC_A8_FAIMS_-55CV_Inj2.raw\nE240906_17_AMC_HSA_340B_142_IV_pepSEC_B8_FAIMS_-55CV_Inj2.raw\nE240906_18_AMC_HSA_340B_142_IV_pepSEC_B7_FAIMS_-55CV_Inj2.raw\nE240906_19_AMC_HSA_340B_142_IV_pepSEC_B6_FAIMS_-55CV_Inj2.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nExpanding the crosslinking mass spectrometry toolbox with vinyl sulfone crosslinkers\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCrosslinking mass spectrometry (MS) is a powerful approach for probing protein structures. However, most widely used crosslinkers rely on N-hydroxysuccinimide (NHS) esters, restricting reactivity primarily to lysine residues and protein N-termini, and rendering them incompatible with many amine-containing buffers (e.g., Tris) and key biochemical cofactors (e.g., ATP). To address these limitations, we introduce two novel vinyl-sulfone-based crosslinkers. Alkyne-BVSC is an enrichable, homobifunctional crosslinker featuring an acid-cleavable alkyne handle for downstream peptide enrichment. VSD is a heterobifunctional crosslinker combining a vinyl sulfone with a diazirine moiety for UV-activated photo-crosslinking. Both reagents are synthetically accessible from inexpensive precursors and retain reactivity in amine-rich biochemical environments. We show that vinyl sulfones react with cysteine, histidine, and lysine residues, thereby expanding crosslinkable residues beyond those accessible to NHS-esters. Moreover, we develop a stub-based post-search filtering strategy that leverages the MS-cleavable nature of vinyl sulfone linkages to improve crosslink identification sensitivity. Together, these advances establish vinyl-sulfone-based crosslinkers as versatile and complementary tools for structural proteomics.\n\n[METHODS]\nProteins were crosslinked as described above, but instead of addition of sample buffer, proteins were precipitated in acetone at -20°C overnight. Pellets were then spun at 15,000xG at 4°C for 10 minutes and acetone was removed via pipette; samples were allowed to air-dry. For samples with large amounts of liquid, samples were spun at 4,000xG. Proteins were resuspended in an 8M/100mM urea/ammonium bicarbonate solution and a BCA assay was used to determine protein concentration; samples were subsequently diluted to 1-3 mg/mL. Proteins were then reduced with dithiothreitol (5mM) for 30 minutes at RT with shaking. Solutions were incubated with iodoacetamide (15mM) for 20 minutes at RT in the dark. Reactions were quenched with re-addition of DTT (5mM) and urea concentration was diluted to >1.5M using 100mM ammonium bicarbonate. Samples were incubated with trypsin (1:50 protease:protein) overnight at RT with shaking. For the HSA crosslinked samples, the Lys-C digest was skipped.  The following day, samples were either: A) acidified with TFA or formic acid (depending on the experiment) to pH 3 and a C18 stage-tip clean-up was performed or B) diluted with PBS and incubated with streptavidin beads for 1 hour at RT with rotation. For the latter, beads were pre-washed with PBS (3 x 10mL, 1,400xG spin for 1 min. at 4°C) and aliquoted as per manufacturer recommendation based on the amount of protein in the sample. Beads were washed with ammonium bicarbonate (25mM, 3 x 10mL, 1,400xG spin for 3 mins.) followed by sterile-filtered water (3 x 10mL, 1,400xG spin for 3 mins.). Beads were transferred to protein lo-bind tubes and liquid was removed after another spin. Beads were then incubated with 50% acetonitrile with 0.1% formic acid for 3 minutes at RT followed by spinning at 1,400xG spin for 3 mins. Supernatant was pipetted into a protein lo-bind tube this process was repeated twice. Samples were dried in vacuo before desalting with C18 stage tips to elution and then drying again in vacuo.  Stage tips[38] were made in-house using Empore SPE Disks by packing three C18 pieces on top of one another in a 200uL pipette tip. Stage tips were activated using 20uL of methanol, and the methanol was washed out twice using 20uL of 80% acetonitrile followed by 2 more washes of 0.1% TFA. All samples were loaded onto the stage tips and washed twice with 20uL of 0.1% TFA. To elute samples from still-wet stage-tips, two elutions of 10uL of 80% acetonitrile were performed. To elute samples from the dry stage tips, 10uL of methanol was pushed almost all the way through the stage tip followed by 2 x 10uL of 80% acetonitrile, with the first elution pushed almost all the way through and the second all the way to complete dryness. Eluted peptides were collected in protein lo-bind tubes and dried in vacuo. Stage tips were never allowed to go to full dryness unless storing washed, loaded sample at -20°C.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
