2026-06-30 15:52:02,816 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-a42d7144-96f3-4240-bf85-1590c8c50c3a', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: sex\nsdrf_column: characteristics[sex]\nontology: PATO:0000047\ndescription: Biological sex of the donor.\nallowed_values: male, female, intersex, hermaphrodite, anonymized, not available, not applicable\ninstructions: Use one of the fixed values above. Return "not available" if not stated.\n  Return "not applicable" if the sample is from a cell line or organism without defined sex.\n\nterm: age\nsdrf_column: characteristics[age]\nontology: EFO:0000246\ndescription: Age of the donor at sample collection.\npattern: {Number}{Unit} where Unit is Y (years), M (months), W (weeks), D (days).\n  Also allowed: "anonymized" if age is stated but anonymized.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: ancestry category\nsdrf_column: characteristics[ancestry category]\nontology: HANCESTRO\ndescription: Ancestry or ethnic background of the donor.\ninstructions: Use HANCESTRO terms if possible. Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: biological replicate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[biological replicate]\nontology: MS:1001809\ndescription: Identifier for the biological replicate (integer or "pooled").\ninstructions: Return a description of the replication strategy from the paper\n  (e.g. "3 biological replicates", "2 independent experiments"). The actual\n  integer values are assigned per-file automatically; describe what you find in the text.\n\nterm: genotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genotype]\nontology: EFO:0000513\ndescription: Known genetic variant, mutation, or genotype of the subject.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Example: "wild type", "Δatg5", "ΔYme1".\n\nterm: pooled sample\nsdrf_column: characteristics[pooled sample]\nontology: NCIT:C165587\ndescription: Whether the sample is a pooled sample.\nallowed_values: not pooled, pooled, or pattern SN=sample1;SN=sample2\ninstructions: Return "not pooled" unless explicitly stated otherwise.\n\nterm: phosphate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phosphate]\nontology: PRIDE:0000805\ndescription: Phosphate concentration (only relevant for environmental/water samples).\npattern: number + mg/L or umol/L\ninstructions: Return null unless this is a water/environmental sample with explicit\n  phosphate concentration stated.\n\nterm: temperature\nsdrf_column: characteristics[temperature]\nontology: PRIDE:0000703\ndescription: Temperature at the sampling location (environmental samples only).\npattern: number + °C\ninstructions: Return null unless this is an environmental/metaproteomics sample\n  with explicit temperature stated. Do NOT confuse with crosslinking temperature.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nExpanding the crosslinking mass spectrometry toolbox with vinyl sulfone crosslinkers\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCrosslinking mass spectrometry (MS) is a powerful approach for probing protein structures. However, most widely used crosslinkers rely on N-hydroxysuccinimide (NHS) esters, restricting reactivity primarily to lysine residues and protein N-termini, and rendering them incompatible with many amine-containing buffers (e.g., Tris) and key biochemical cofactors (e.g., ATP). To address these limitations, we introduce two novel vinyl-sulfone-based crosslinkers. Alkyne-BVSC is an enrichable, homobifunctional crosslinker featuring an acid-cleavable alkyne handle for downstream peptide enrichment. VSD is a heterobifunctional crosslinker combining a vinyl sulfone with a diazirine moiety for UV-activated photo-crosslinking. Both reagents are synthetically accessible from inexpensive precursors and retain reactivity in amine-rich biochemical environments. We show that vinyl sulfones react with cysteine, histidine, and lysine residues, thereby expanding crosslinkable residues beyond those accessible to NHS-esters. Moreover, we develop a stub-based post-search filtering strategy that leverages the MS-cleavable nature of vinyl sulfone linkages to improve crosslink identification sensitivity. Together, these advances establish vinyl-sulfone-based crosslinkers as versatile and complementary tools for structural proteomics.\n\n[METHODS]\nProteins were crosslinked as described above, but instead of addition of sample buffer, proteins were precipitated in acetone at -20°C overnight. Pellets were then spun at 15,000xG at 4°C for 10 minutes and acetone was removed via pipette; samples were allowed to air-dry. For samples with large amounts of liquid, samples were spun at 4,000xG. Proteins were resuspended in an 8M/100mM urea/ammonium bicarbonate solution and a BCA assay was used to determine protein concentration; samples were subsequently diluted to 1-3 mg/mL. Proteins were then reduced with dithiothreitol (5mM) for 30 minutes at RT with shaking. Solutions were incubated with iodoacetamide (15mM) for 20 minutes at RT in the dark. Reactions were quenched with re-addition of DTT (5mM) and urea concentration was diluted to >1.5M using 100mM ammonium bicarbonate. Samples were incubated with trypsin (1:50 protease:protein) overnight at RT with shaking. For the HSA crosslinked samples, the Lys-C digest was skipped.  The following day, samples were either: A) acidified with TFA or formic acid (depending on the experiment) to pH 3 and a C18 stage-tip clean-up was performed or B) diluted with PBS and incubated with streptavidin beads for 1 hour at RT with rotation. For the latter, beads were pre-washed with PBS (3 x 10mL, 1,400xG spin for 1 min. at 4°C) and aliquoted as per manufacturer recommendation based on the amount of protein in the sample. Beads were washed with ammonium bicarbonate (25mM, 3 x 10mL, 1,400xG spin for 3 mins.) followed by sterile-filtered water (3 x 10mL, 1,400xG spin for 3 mins.). Beads were transferred to protein lo-bind tubes and liquid was removed after another spin. Beads were then incubated with 50% acetonitrile with 0.1% formic acid for 3 minutes at RT followed by spinning at 1,400xG spin for 3 mins. Supernatant was pipetted into a protein lo-bind tube this process was repeated twice. Samples were dried in vacuo before desalting with C18 stage tips to elution and then drying again in vacuo.  Stage tips[38] were made in-house using Empore SPE Disks by packing three C18 pieces on top of one another in a 200uL pipette tip. Stage tips were activated using 20uL of methanol, and the methanol was washed out twice using 20uL of 80% acetonitrile followed by 2 more washes of 0.1% TFA. All samples were loaded onto the stage tips and washed twice with 20uL of 0.1% TFA. To elute samples from still-wet stage-tips, two elutions of 10uL of 80% acetonitrile were performed. To elute samples from the dry stage tips, 10uL of methanol was pushed almost all the way through the stage tip followed by 2 x 10uL of 80% acetonitrile, with the first elution pushed almost all the way through and the second all the way to complete dryness. Eluted peptides were collected in protein lo-bind tubes and dried in vacuo. Stage tips were never allowed to go to full dryness unless storing washed, loaded sample at -20°C.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
